	(text_box "Design needs to incorporate a synchronisation mechanism to minimise the impact of a slower CPU clock.\n\nWhile the CPU clock is less than 1/4 of the video memory clock:\n\nlatch the read/write state on request, the actual request only takes place after the next memory request (this needs testing as in 2bpp a memory request couold take 16 clock cycles to complete which is *way* outside the host's response window)\nlatch the response when it comes for the duration of the remaining read/write request. This is really two separate mechanisms as a write doesn't need a response and it is just a matter of getting the write to take place out of phase with the video reads\n\nI suspect (to prove) what is needed is something adaptive to BPP and CPP with a counter that identifies a safe iDMA window. The actual read/write only needs a single master clock cycle. With a Dragon running at 0.89MHz that means the master clock is running 16 times faster than the host. The actual access window is half a clock cycle but that is still 8 MCCs. The fastest video access is at 1CPP and 4BPP at which point the memory access cycle is 4 MCC... finding an available access slot should not be difficult. We really need a minimum of 2 MCC ticks to be safe\n\nAll of this would be unnecessary if the host's raw clock was exposed but the best we can hope for is an XOR of the quadrature clock to give 1.78MHz"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
