		(uuid "9c609977-80dc-40b3-af68-bd20741f717e")
	)
	(text_box "The host address only needs to be applied for a single master clock cycle in order to achieve a complete read or write from the video system perspective\n\nThe host must see the read data persisted to the end of the active E pulse which will be approximately 8 cycles\n\nIt is also possible the host is in \"fast\" mode in which case a memory access duration drops from 8 cycles to 4. The premise here is that the system cannot attempt to predict how long it has to complete. It must perform the operation in the shortest possible time\n\nThis negates the need for counting cycles or knowing how wide the access window is. The process either happens immediately in the same (14MHz cycle) or it is blocked until the following cycle.\n\nThe process cannot begin until there is a falling edge of the main clock to synchronise the process. At that point the process is either free or blocked by the video access.\nRegardless of blocking state the address required is latched at the earliest opportunity. Data is latched on write\n\nThe memory access takes place at cycle n or n+1. The outcome of a read must be latched and held for the duration of E being held high. Regardless of blocking state the read data will be presented to the host for at least 2 cycles and most importantly those are the last cycles of the access where the host expects the result to be stable"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 288.29 13.97 0)
		(size 63.5 64.77)
		(margins 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525 0.9525)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type solid)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "b5b884b8-841a-47ee-8f61-6f6975a5627b")
	)
